Enable GPU timer queries on GLES, and populate per-view stats.

Two gaps rather than customisations:

1. bgfx reports no GPU timing on any GLES device. glQueryCounter,
   glGetQueryObjectiv, glGetQueryObjecti64v and glGetQueryObjectui64v are not
   core in GLES -- they come from EXT_disjoint_timer_query with EXT suffixes,
   and that is the only spelling a GLES driver exports. The dynamic-import
   block asks for the unsuffixed names only, so the pointers stay null,
   m_timerQuerySupport is forced false, and Stats::gpuTime* is always zero.

   Adding an EXT line after each core line is enough: GL_EXTENSION only assigns
   when the pointer is still NULL, so desktop GL keeps the core entry point and
   GLES falls through to the EXT one. Note this must go in the
   BGFX_USE_GL_DYNAMIC_LIB block -- the GLES-only block further down is
   #elif !BGFX_USE_GL_DYNAMIC_LIB, which is dead on every platform that loads
   GL through EGL/GLX/WGL, and there GL_EXTENSION resolves to a direct
   declaration rather than a dlsym.

2. Stats::viewStats and Stats::numViews are public API but no backend has ever
   populated them. TimerQueryGL::m_result is already sized BGFX_CONFIG_MAX_VIEWS+1
   and begin() already takes a result slot, yet every backend passes only the
   whole-frame slot. Time each view into its own slot and publish the results, so
   getStats() gives per-view GPU attribution.

   Exactly one view is timed per frame, round-robin. Timing every view is not
   viable on a tile-based GPU: a timestamp between two views sharing a
   framebuffer stops the driver merging them into a single render pass, forcing
   a tile flush and reload at each boundary. On Mali-G610 that turned a 26 ms
   frame into multi-second stalls. One view per frame costs two timestamps and
   gives the same attribution across a few frames.

